On or about 5/30/2004 7:06 PM, Kristoffer Björkman did proclaim:
> 
> If you want 'IBMgraphics' in PuTTY with the Terminal font, here's how I 
> do it...
> 
> Related Nethack settings:
> OPTIONS=color,IBMgraphics,eight_bit_tty,use_inverse
> BOULDER=233
> (BOULDER because I like my boulders to look like boulders, I'm not sure 
> if the 8bit option really matters, but that's how I have it set)
> 
> PuTTY settings:
> Window -> Appearance -> Font: Terminal
> Window -> Translation -> Received data assumed to...: Use font encoding
> (everything else can be left to default settings)

 From PuTTY's help:
   If you want the old IBM PC character set with block graphics
   and line-drawing characters, you can select 'CP437'.
-and-
   'Unicode mode' tries to use the box characters that are present
   in Unicode. For good Unicode-supporting fonts this is probably
   the most reliable and functional option.

That said, here are some (IMHO) better PuTTY settings:

   Window -> Appearance -> Font: Courier New, Bold, 10 pt
   Window -> Translation ->
     Received data assumed to be...: CP437
     Handling of line drawing characters: Unicode mode

Courier New is a 'good Unicode-supporting font', so this sets up all the 
  IBMgraphics characters.  (For the last option, 'Unicode mode' is the 
default, but I thought I'd best be specific about it.)


